**Use Lake as a scalpel.** When your scope requires Lean
elaboration, build the narrowest module that can answer the
question. Prefer:

```shell
timeout 60s lake -q build +Module.Name
```

`-q` hides cached informational messages and progress while
preserving warnings and errors. Drop it only when the messages
are the evidence you need. Do not start with `lake build`, a
library umbrella, or an entire research area when one module
will do.

**Trust the workspace boundary.** The harness gives every agent
its own mutable project build cache and safely symlinks the
partially unchanging parts of `.lake`. Do not add `flock`, copy
build trees, point `LEAN_PATH` into another workspace, delete
`.lake`, or manufacture another cache layout.

**Bound every probe.** Begin with a short external timeout. A
normal single-module check gets about 60 seconds; a known cold
finite computation may justify about 300 seconds. Exit 124 is a
timeout, not a proof failure. Inspect what was rebuilding before
raising one bound, and never retry the unchanged command merely
because it timed out.

Use only the resources the check needs. For a one-file probe,
start Lean with one thread and a deliberate memory ceiling, for
example:

```shell
timeout 60s lake env lean -j 1 -M 4000 Proofs/Scratch/Probe.lean
```

`-M` is in megabytes. Raise it only after an observed allocation
failure. If a Lake build does not need the whole host, constrain
it to an available CPU set with `taskset`; choose the set from
current machine state rather than blindly pinning every agent to
CPU 0. Long checks belong in `tool_task`, not a blocked foreground
shell.

**Respect module caches.** Lean caches modules, not individual
theorems. A large `decide`, `bv_decide`, or expanded finite check
is paid again whenever its containing source module changes.
Keep stable computation in a small dependency module and put
changing semantic bridges downstream. A harmless edit to the
computation module breaks that cache boundary and can make both
the editor and the next build lag; do not restart competing
builds while the bounded cold check is still running.

Use `lake env lean` for disposable one-file probes and explicit
audits. It re-elaborates that source file and is a poor edit-build
loop for a large production module. Use the narrow Lake target
above for normal incremental work so unchanged imports come from
the workspace cache.
